      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Run-time error '1004' in that context, especially when it's intermittent, is often related to how Excel is *seeing* the range, even if it looks fine to you. It's less about the code logic and more about the state of the spreadsheet itself. Think of it like a very cranky, unpredictable gremlin hiding in the file structure.\n\nThe most common culprit when things suddenly go sideways in a specific file is often a hidden calculation issue or a lingering object reference. Even if the sheet isn't protected, sometimes underlying worksheet settings or external links can cause this. Try setting `Application.ScreenUpdating = False` at the very start of your macro and `True` at the end; this can sometimes stabilize volatile operations.\n\nAlso, since you mentioned it works in a new file, the issue might be tied to named ranges or external data connections within that specific workbook. If there are any complex formulas or pivot tables referencing external sources, those can sometimes trigger this error when you try to clear a large range. Try running the macro after manually saving the file as a completely new `.xlsx` file to see if that sheds any light on the corruption.",
